true}],"intro":{"body":"Kilsyth is a town on Clydeside, historically part of Lanarkshire, in the Central Belt of Scotland. It's 15 miles northeast of Glasgow near the watershed between the Clyde and Forth river catchments, at a point where the natural lowland routes become pinched between the Lanarkshire moors, the abrupt Campsie Fells, and sucking bogs. So this way have passed the Romans, cattle droves, warring armies (the Battle of Kilsyth was fought here in 1645), and later road, canal and rail routes. The town claims to be the birthplace of the ice-sport of curling and to host the oldest club, though there are stronger contenders for both titles.","grounding":[{"source":"wikivoyage","title":"Kilsyth","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Kilsyth","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"short":"Kilsyth is a small town northeast of Glasgow where the Antonine Wall, a World Heritage Site, runs past. The Campsie Fells rise just beyond the houses and Culcreuch Castle is a short drive off. Its best run is June to August, six hours in all.","short_src":"rewrite","facts":{"population":9860,"visit_h":5.8}},"highlights":[{"id":"hl-antonine-wall","name":"Antonine Wall","kind":"Ruins","lat":55.967,"lon":-4.067,"dist_km":1.1,"rank_score":10.53,"resolved_by":"catalogue","fact":"Defensive fortification in Roman Britain","wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonine_Wall"},{"id":"hl-campsie-fells","name":"Campsie Fells","kind":"Peak","lat":56.01667,"lon":-4.3,"dist_km":15.6,"rank_score":7.22,"resolved_by":"catalogue","image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Campsielong.JPG/960px-Campsielong.JPG","author":"MacRusgail at English Wikipedia","licence":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campsielong.JPG","w":1602,"h":546,"ok_print":true}},{"id":"hl-broadwood-stadium","name":"Broadwood Stadium","kind":"Landmark","lat":55.9448,"lon":-4.03709,"dist_km":3.7,"rank_score":6.7,"resolved_by":"catalogue"},{"id":"hl-culcreuch-castle","name